MODEL WEIGHTS
-------------

The SSDLite320 MobileNetV3-Large detector uses the COCO_V1 pretrained weights
distributed with torchvision under BSD-3-Clause. The weights are downloaded
from torchvision at run time and are not redistributed here. Their sha256 is
pinned in configs/protocol_v1.yaml so that a reviewer can confirm the exact
checkpoint used.


DATASET
-------

Evaluation uses the COCO 2017 validation split. Images and annotations are
downloaded from cocodataset.org at run time by scripts/00_fetch_coco.sh and are
not redistributed here.

COCO annotations are made available under CC BY 4.0. The images themselves are
subject to the terms under which their original Flickr contributors licensed
them; COCO distributes them for non-commercial research use and does not own
their copyright.

This repository contains only image *identifiers* -- the pool manifests in
configs/manifests/ list COCO image IDs, not image data.


ATTACK COMPONENTS: WHAT IS INCLUDED AND WHY
-------------------------------------------

The project's dissemination policy reserves the right to release in limited
form, withhold, or share only with qualified reviewers any attack component
that would materially reduce the difficulty of targeting realistic deployed
systems, and to identify what was limited and why.

Nothing has been withheld from this release. The reasoning:

  1. The person-suppression patch here is an independent reimplementation of a
     method published in 2019 (Thys, Van Ranst and Goedeme, CVPRW 2019), for
     which reference implementations have been publicly available since then.
     It introduces no technique that is not already in the open literature.

  2. It is optimized against a specific public pretrained checkpoint on a
     general-purpose object-detection dataset. It is not tuned to any deployed
     system, vendor product, camera model, or operational environment.

  3. It is evaluated only digitally, under simulated transformations. No patch
     was printed, photographed, or tested physically, and no claim is made that
     the digital result transfers to a physical setting.

  4. The artifact's purpose is measurement. Publishing the attack alongside the
     evaluation protocol is what allows an independent party to reproduce and
     challenge the reported numbers; withholding it would leave the defensive
     claims unfalsifiable.

This assessment covers the artifact as released. It should be revisited before
any future release that adds physical-world patches, transfer attacks against
undisclosed models, or evaluation against a specific deployed system.


